# base.apt — native/system packages (apt). NO Python security tools here.
# One package per line; comments with '#'. Consumed by modules/00-base.sh.

# --- core cli / build ---
build-essential
git
# gh: modules 90 and 99 both tell you to run 'gh auth login', and 90 uses it to
# upload the new SSH key — but nothing installed it, so those instructions
# pointed at a binary that was never on the box. It is guarded by `have gh`,
# which is why the gap stayed quiet through a full provision.
gh
curl
wget
ca-certificates
gnupg
jq
unzip
p7zip-full
ripgrep
fd-find
bat
fzf
tmux
zsh
neovim
xxd
xclip
rlwrap
socat
proxychains4

# --- networking / recon (native) ---
net-tools
iproute2
dnsutils
whois
tcpdump
nmap
masscan
netcat-openbsd
smbclient
cifs-utils
nfs-common
ldap-utils
openvpn
wireguard-tools

# --- cracking (native) ---
hashcat
hydra
john

# --- native toolchain -------------------------------------------------------
# The 32-bit multilib packages (gcc-multilib, g++-multilib, libc6-dev-i386)
# deliberately do NOT live here. They only exist for x86_64, and on arm64 with
# the i386 architecture enabled apt "satisfies" them with :i386 builds that
# displace the native compiler. They are installed, arch-guarded, by module 60.
libc6-dev
libssl-dev
libffi-dev

# --- headers for Python packages with no wheel on this arch/interpreter ---
# cmake is required to build unicorn (a pwntools dependency). The xml/curl
# headers are kept for any source build that needs lxml or pycurl — nothing in
# the lists needs them today, but they are cheap and their absence produces
# very unobvious wheel-build errors.
libxml2-dev
libxslt1-dev
libcurl4-openssl-dev
cmake

# --- language runtimes (go/rust/ruby installed separately in 00-base) ---
ruby
ruby-dev
npm
python3
python3-dev
python3-venv
python3-pip
pipx

# --- java for ghidra / jadx / burp ---
openjdk-21-jdk
default-jdk

# --- wordlists ---
# 'seclists' and 'wordlists' are Kali-only packages and do not exist in the
# Ubuntu archive. SecLists is cloned from GitHub in 00-base.sh instead, which
# also supplies rockyou.txt (the 'wordlists' package's only real draw here).
